      "math_academy_id": "q-159843",
      "problem": "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3},{X}_{4}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼Po(\\theta )$ with unknown rate parameter $\\theta .$ You're given the particular sample\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=4,\\,{x}_{2}=1,\\,{x}_{3}=0,\\,{x}_{4}=2.\n$$\n\nThe log-likelihood function of the sample is given by\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=a\\theta +b\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{ln}⁡c.\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$\n\n*Hint: The probability mass function for a Poisson distribution is given by* \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\theta }^{x}{e}^{-\\theta }}{x!},\\,x=0,1,2,…\n$$",
